      "math_academy_id": "q-112935",
      "sequence_position": 13,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/70c4581f3b802172e5d66a514eda3a37293f7ab2bf65ef6dcccb8073832e5f96.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=2f(x-3)?$",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=2f(x-3),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Translate it right by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=f(x-3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/c9ac259b9e9a907a8c00053bdb1818f44226f126b3544358dc98c1329a5617af.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2f(x-3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/757800fd367f695175be1cb991b62ae0a576eec9ee1e0018a08d56c945ec6de7.png)",
